README-NAMES -- names here that are names you already haveSome programs on this disk carry the name of a utility your OS-9 camewith.  Nothing here overwrites anything; this is a disk you mount.  Twothings decide which program a name runs.WHAT DECIDES------------  1. Your execution search.  A bare `dir' is looked for in the execution     directory and then along your PATH, in order.  Put this disk's CMDS     ahead of yours and this disk's `dir' answers; put it after and yours     does.  A full pathlist -- `/h0/CMDS/dir' -- runs that file.  2. Residency.  Once a module is in memory, OS-9 finds it by module     name, whatever path you type.  `load' one of these and it answers     for that name everywhere until `unlink' lets it go -- the same     mechanism DOC/README-RUNNING uses for programs that fork a helper by     bare name.  `mdir' lists what is resident: look there when a     familiar command starts behaving unfamiliarly.THE NAMES---------Each line is what this disk's program is.  chown      set the user half of a file's owner, by number  compress   compress and uncompress, Lempel-Ziv-Welch  cpp        Decus CPP, a C preprocessor  dir        a directory listing with its own options (`-e' adds more)  dump       a hex dump of a file, or of a module in memory (-m)  expand     GNU expand: tabs to spaces  grep       GNU grep 2.0  help       a help system reading its articles from SYS/HELP  kermit     OS-9 Kermit 1.5, serial file transfer  make       a make that maintains a target  mshell     a menu shell  mv         GNU mv (fileutils 3.13)  printenv   print the environment  setime     set the system time, prompting YYMMDDHHMMSS  su         GNU su  tar        a 1989 tar (tar.c v1.5)  umacs      uMacs 1.0 -- MicroEMACS in 45K  undel      bring back a deleted file  what       inventory the expansion cards in a GEPARDIn subdirectories, reached only if you put them on your PATH: `cc1plus',`cc2', `cc2plus', `cccp2', `collect', `gcc2' and `gpp' in CMDS/GCC2 andCMDS/GCC139, and `inetd' in CMDS/WN.  DOC/README-GCC is about the firstseven.  The gcc passes installed in CMDS carry four of those names asmodule names -- gcc_cccp2 and gpp_cccp are cccp2, gcc_cc2 is cc2,gpp_cc1plus is cc1plus, gpp_collect is collect -- so `load' one and thatname is resident.NOT SHADOWS-----------`cio', `csl', `csl020', `math' and `math881' are Microware's ownmodules, here by permission: the same thing under the same name.SOURCES.txt records the permission.HOW THIS LIST WAS MADE----------------------Every filename under this disk's CMDS was compared with the utility setof an OS-9/68K system disk.  A system older or newer than that one willdiffer a little at the edges; the two rules above do not change.